    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1417">

                <pb facs="1094" n="978" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg978"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.512/777.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Special Agent Carothers to the Secretary of
                    State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">El Paso</hi></placeName>, (<date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1915-08-26T23:59:59-07:00"
                            notBefore="1915-08-23T00:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Undated</hi></date><hi rendition="#italic">; received August 26,
                            1915</hi>).</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your August 23. It is impossible at this time to get cotton tax removed. From
                    information given me by employees of Tlahualilo <pb facs="1095" n="979"
                        xml:id="pg979"/> hacienda they are so far not suffering very much except for
                    certain amounts of corn taken for troops. I have protested to General Villa
                    against this seizure and asking for full protection of property. There is little
                    hope of securing a removal of the tax and I believe it will be best for them not
                    to ship until some definite arrangement can be made.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Carothers.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
